WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.720
-Yes. So today, 
we are talking about

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.440
normalized device
coordinate space.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.260
-That sounds cool.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:08.760
-I know.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.640
I just call it NDC
space because I'm lazy.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.640
-I think we all 
call it just NDC.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.640
-Yes. But it's made up
these three long words.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.140
Let's start with the first one.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:19.640
Normalized.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.140
-Means it's has a length of one.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.640
-A length of one.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.963
Yeah. Because we normalize
vectors and things.

00:00:24.963 --> 00:00:27.140
Device.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:27.890
-This is the actual
memory in the

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:28.640
GPU that's going 
to end up on the

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:30.140
computer screen.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:31.867
And we're going to 
normalize that

00:00:31.867 --> 00:00:34.984
space, that region of memory, 
so

00:00:34.984 --> 00:00:37.461
that it is one in the X
dimension

00:00:37.461 --> 00:00:38.697
and negative one back

00:00:38.697 --> 00:00:40.830
in the negative X dimension.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:44.670
It's going to be positive
up or Y is going to be up.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.435
That's going to be positive one.

00:00:46.435 --> 00:00:47.495
And then down it'll go negative

00:00:47.495 --> 00:00:48.555
one, and then there's 
going to be

00:00:48.555 --> 00:00:50.675
the in and out 
on the z axis.

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:53.155
Same thing, so it
sort of makes a cube.

00:00:53.155 --> 00:00:54.995
-Oh, and we have a cube.

00:00:54.995 --> 00:00:56.515
Thank you to Cassidy.

00:00:56.515 --> 00:00:59.900
Look at this beautiful
NDC cube we have here.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.740
Yeah. And you'll notice it kind 
of looks like the scene that

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.580
Cassidy has set up for us.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.580
At the front, there's
this huge croissant.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.500
So much bigger than what
you have at the back.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.580
You've got this tiny looking
chair, and it's kind of

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.420
like what we have here.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:14.920
Look at that croissant.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.695
It looks the size of the

00:01:16.695 --> 00:01:18.195
couch.

00:01:18.195 --> 00:01:19.695
-Yeah, that's perspective.

00:01:19.695 --> 00:01:21.425
So the way we're
going to make the

00:01:21.425 --> 00:01:23.155
perspective view 
work is to take

00:01:23.155 --> 00:01:25.475
a space that we're going to

00:01:25.475 --> 00:01:28.915
be drawing into and
then distort that space.

00:01:28.915 --> 00:01:32.890
So I'm going to draw this
thing into a truncated pyramid.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:35.970
To do that I can expand the
outer part of this thing, shrink

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:39.050
the inner part of it just
mathematically.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:40.641
Of course it's not really

00:01:40.641 --> 00:01:42.232
changing but just 
mathematically.

00:01:42.232 --> 00:01:45.415
Make that look smaller and the
other part look bigger.

00:01:45.415 --> 00:01:47.975
So we'll make a little
front window here.

00:01:47.975 --> 00:01:50.535
This part back here, it
gets expanded all out.

00:01:50.535 --> 00:01:52.435
All right.

00:01:52.435 --> 00:01:55.302
And then when I render
objects into the cube, things

00:01:55.302 --> 00:01:59.270
that are in the back look like
they're in a huge, big volume.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:01.250
Follow that?

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:02.750
So they're in a big volume.

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:04.630
And so they look very
small in that big volume.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.130
-Yes. Yeah.

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:07.630
That couch looks very small now.

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:09.167
-Right. And then objects up

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:12.055
close are rendered into what
looks like a small volume.

00:02:12.055 --> 00:02:13.975
So they look much larger.

00:02:13.975 --> 00:02:16.935
And that's the role
of the projection matrix.

00:02:16.935 --> 00:02:18.615
So then the projection matrix,

00:02:18.615 --> 00:02:20.295
we're going to go 
into perspective.

00:02:20.295 --> 00:02:23.723
-Yes. So this is NDC space and
you're right to get here, we

00:02:23.723 --> 00:02:27.150
hopped over from camera space.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.910
And in camera space, yeah, you
need the projection matrix.

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:33.870
And we did a perspective divide
to make sure things look bigger

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:36.350
at the front.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:39.001
-Well, that's, yeah, the
perspective divide is because

00:02:39.001 --> 00:02:41.653
we've got to get back into
three-dimensional space because

00:02:41.653 --> 00:02:44.304
the projection matrix is going
to take us into a four

00:02:44.304 --> 00:02:47.275
dimensional distorted space.

00:02:47.275 --> 00:02:50.853
So dividing by the W component
is going to go from four

00:02:50.853 --> 00:02:54.395
dimensions back down to three
dimensions.

00:02:54.395 --> 00:02:57.710
Because then as we've discussed
before,

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.350
the W needs to be one.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:02.830
-Yes. My brain can
only cope in three d.

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:05.075
When you start talking about
four d,

00:03:05.075 --> 00:03:06.835
I feel like I'm losing
my mind.

00:03:06.835 --> 00:03:10.755
-Well, it's just 
four d for fun.

00:03:10.755 --> 00:03:12.835
We're just so we can just
distort things, but the

00:03:12.835 --> 00:03:15.750
distortion will occur in the
fourth dimension.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:18.310
And dividing through by the w,

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:20.870
the w divided by w is a one.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:23.110
So now you're satisfied
because it's one.

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:24.647
And now that the 
x and y and z

00:03:24.647 --> 00:03:26.036
coordinates are all 
going to look

00:03:26.036 --> 00:03:28.396
like we're looking into a
perspective view like Cassidy

00:03:28.396 --> 00:03:30.310
set up for us.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:31.810
-Oh, thank you.

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:35.515
And also, Cassidy set up the
camera view here in Unreal.

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:37.835
Looks a little different to the
usual seat we're at

00:03:37.835 --> 00:03:40.155
in Cassidy's coffee shop.

00:03:40.155 --> 00:03:42.475
We're off a little
bit to the side.

00:03:42.475 --> 00:03:45.127
And you'll notice the box that
Cassidy made, that's the stuff

00:03:45.127 --> 00:03:47.780
that's rendered.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:50.420
So you only render
things are in the box.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:52.340
I mean, there could be
stuff outside the box, just like

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:54.500
in the screen.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.763
There could be stuff on the
left, right, top, down, but you

00:03:56.763 --> 00:03:59.025
don't want to render that stuff.

00:03:59.025 --> 00:04:00.985
-Everything that you see in a
video game is occurring inside

00:04:00.985 --> 00:04:02.945
your little box.

00:04:02.945 --> 00:04:04.845
-Wow. Yeah.

00:04:04.845 --> 00:04:06.625
So this box, let's see.

00:04:06.625 --> 00:04:08.125
How big is it?

00:04:08.125 --> 00:04:09.625
-The origin's in

00:04:09.625 --> 00:04:11.125
the centre of the box,
right in the middle,

00:04:11.125 --> 00:04:12.847
in OpenGL.

00:04:12.847 --> 00:04:14.360
-Hold on, what's OpenGL?

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:16.650
-OpenGL is a graphics

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:18.670
API, very popular.

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:21.830
In the OpenGL and Vulkan world,
the origin is at the centre in

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:25.735
there of the normalized device
coordinate box.

00:04:25.735 --> 00:04:29.055
And it goes one off to the
right, one off to the left, one

00:04:29.055 --> 00:04:32.375
up, one down, one in, one out.

00:04:32.375 --> 00:04:33.895
Another one is DirectX.

00:04:33.895 --> 00:04:36.215
And of course Microsoft had
to do it a little different.

00:04:36.215 --> 00:04:39.530
The origin is at the
bottom left hand corner.

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:43.370
And then the far diagonal
corner is a one by one by one.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:45.050
They don't go into
any negative numbers.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:48.628
And so that just means that
the perspective matrix has to be

00:04:48.628 --> 00:04:52.205
completely re-derived for that
math to work.

00:04:52.205 --> 00:04:54.658
It ends up being the same thing,
but it just it's a lot more work

00:04:54.658 --> 00:04:57.112
in DirectX, unless you're a
DirectX fan, in which case

00:04:57.112 --> 00:04:59.565
OpenGL is being the bother.

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:02.373
-I like OpenGL because it cross
platform works

00:05:02.373 --> 00:05:05.180
with any device you have.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:06.680
-Everybody. 
-Yeah.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:08.180
I love OpenGL.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.940
So yes, the OpenGL NDC box.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:13.500
Yeah. It has a width of two, a

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.060
height of two, 
and a depth of two.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:18.425
And the number two always throws
me off because this is supposed

00:05:18.425 --> 00:05:20.185
to be normalized, right?

00:05:20.185 --> 00:05:21.785
And I think of the number one.

00:05:21.785 --> 00:05:25.225
-See, that's why I kept
not saying it's too wide.

00:05:25.225 --> 00:05:28.433
The origin's at the centre,
and

00:05:28.433 --> 00:05:31.640
it's a one by one by one 
by one box.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:33.880
It is normalized.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:35.380
-Yes. Okay.

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:38.280
So if I think about the planes
of this box, so the top plane,

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.421
that is the Y equal to one.

00:05:41.421 --> 00:05:43.400
That's at the top.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.505
And on the bottom of the
box, Y equal to negative one.

00:05:46.505 --> 00:05:50.185
Okay. The left and
right. Okay, let's see.

00:05:50.185 --> 00:05:53.145
So the right would be
positive one for x.

00:05:53.145 --> 00:05:56.105
The left would be
negative one for x.

00:05:56.105 --> 00:05:58.345
The thing that gets me is the z.

00:05:58.345 --> 00:06:00.441
-We're looking into the computer

00:06:00.441 --> 00:06:02.537
screen down the negative Z axis,

00:06:02.537 --> 00:06:04.634
positive Z then is

00:06:04.634 --> 00:06:06.730
coming out at you.

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:08.330
-Yes, yes.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:10.330
I'm thinking about the
right hand rule right now.

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:13.797
Whenever you see me doing this,
I'm thinking, okay, there's my

00:06:13.797 --> 00:06:17.265
X, there's my y, z.

00:06:17.265 --> 00:06:18.765
Okay, yes.

00:06:18.765 --> 00:06:20.465
Z goes towards you.

00:06:20.465 --> 00:06:22.065
Now for some reason,
and I want to

00:06:22.065 --> 00:06:23.665
blame you for this, 
the NDC box

00:06:23.665 --> 00:06:26.865
is left handed.

00:06:26.865 --> 00:06:29.040
Like this.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:31.360
Yeah. So do it like this.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:35.120
So X still goes to the
right, Y still goes up.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:38.240
But Z is pointing
towards my nose now.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:41.920
So negative Z is
the front plane.

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:43.965
-Oops.

00:06:43.965 --> 00:06:47.085
So that occurred a long time ago
and we've just been living with

00:06:47.085 --> 00:06:50.205
it until the latest release of,
well it's not called OpenGL

00:06:50.205 --> 00:06:53.325
anymore, now it's called Vulkan.

00:06:53.325 --> 00:06:55.499
And then Vulkan fixed it by

00:06:55.499 --> 00:06:57.673
making, see if you can do this

00:06:57.673 --> 00:06:59.688
one with your fingers, 
they made

00:06:59.688 --> 00:07:01.702
the y, instead of y going up

00:07:01.702 --> 00:07:05.433
positive, y goes up 
negative now.

00:07:05.433 --> 00:07:08.625
-Oh. That kind of reminds
me of pixel space.

00:07:08.625 --> 00:07:10.585
Eventually,
we're going to get to

00:07:10.585 --> 00:07:12.545
the screen, 
and there y is going down.

00:07:12.545 --> 00:07:15.025
So you think that's what
Vulkan was thinking?

00:07:15.025 --> 00:07:17.345
-No. I think they just corrected
it, and they're right hand rule

00:07:17.345 --> 00:07:19.665
for the normalized device
coordinate box.

00:07:19.665 --> 00:07:22.292
And then what a lot of us
do that's crazy, we flip it back

00:07:22.292 --> 00:07:26.120
around in the shader.

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:29.560
So, you know, Paul mentioned
this in one of our conversations

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:33.000
that it's kind of arbitrary how
we set all this stuff up.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:34.500
We just need to be consistent.

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:37.240
-Thank you for fixing all the
mess that we make.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:39.135
-It's what I do.

00:07:39.135 --> 00:07:42.735
-Personally, I really need to
know what the Z is because a lot

00:07:42.735 --> 00:07:46.415
of times I'm not going from NDC
space to pixel space.

00:07:46.415 --> 00:07:50.415
In my courses, I have like
a mouse click on the screen.

00:07:50.415 --> 00:07:53.293
So I got the mouse click in
pixel space and I want to get it

00:07:53.293 --> 00:07:56.170
into NDC space.

00:07:56.170 --> 00:07:58.730
And I like to click on the

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:01.210
front of the...
 What is it?

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:02.710
Like, front plane?

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:04.210
-Front clipping plane.

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:05.710
-Front clipping plane.

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:07.210
-So the front clipping plane.

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:09.628
So I have to set the NDC's Z
coordinate to negative one, just

00:08:09.628 --> 00:08:13.056
so that my little mouse
position

00:08:13.056 --> 00:08:16.767
is at the front of my scene.

00:08:16.767 --> 00:08:20.251
So we get here
from camera space.

00:08:20.251 --> 00:08:22.980
So there's a bunch
of moves to get here.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:25.500
There's the perspective
transformation, or we call it

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.260
projection matrix.

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:31.678
There's the perspective
divide to make things bigger

00:08:31.678 --> 00:08:35.095
front, smaller back, and then
you're the NDC box.

00:08:35.095 --> 00:08:38.775
But the next stage over is the
one we're looking at behind us.

00:08:38.775 --> 00:08:42.295
So I really feel like the NDC
box looks the most similar to

00:08:42.295 --> 00:08:45.495
what we actually want on the
screen.

00:08:45.495 --> 00:08:46.995
-This is what we want.

00:08:46.995 --> 00:08:48.495
That is what you're
seeing on the screen.

00:08:48.495 --> 00:08:50.589
-Wow. So when you're 
setting up

00:08:50.589 --> 00:08:52.683
the NDC box, 
I imagine from the

00:08:52.683 --> 00:08:55.229
projection matrix, 
you need to

00:08:55.229 --> 00:08:58.710
know the aspect ratio 
of your window.

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:01.030
-And I'm going to pull that up
from what's called the viewport.

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:03.965
The viewport is the window
that I'm looking at.

00:09:03.965 --> 00:09:06.645
And this will be flattened one
more time by dividing through by

00:09:06.645 --> 00:09:09.085
z this time.

00:09:09.085 --> 00:09:10.685
And then that divides by z.

00:09:10.685 --> 00:09:12.685
That brings you to a
two dimensional space.

00:09:12.685 --> 00:09:15.777
And that's what we're
looking at because these things

00:09:15.777 --> 00:09:18.870
are computer screens and they're
necessarily two dimensional.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:22.550
-Yeah. So do we use the
Z information at all?

00:09:22.550 --> 00:09:23.803
-Yeah. I save that 
just so I know

00:09:23.803 --> 00:09:25.057
how deep something is, 
rendering

00:09:25.057 --> 00:09:27.563
things so that the objects in
the back

00:09:27.563 --> 00:09:30.070
are obscured by objects
up front.

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:31.570
And how do I know
how to do that?

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:33.725
Well, we keep track
of how deep it was.

00:09:33.725 --> 00:09:36.245
-Oh, because 
I see this croissant

00:09:36.245 --> 00:09:38.765
is in front of the 
back wall there.

00:09:38.765 --> 00:09:42.285
-If I didn't keep track of that
in what's called a depth buffer,

00:09:42.285 --> 00:09:45.805
then the back wall might be in
front of the croissant.

00:09:45.805 --> 00:09:48.708
And that would look bad.

00:09:48.708 --> 00:09:51.140
-Yeah. That would be weird.

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:53.220
-So we just keep track of it, 
but in order to

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:54.720
project it onto the
computer screen,

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:56.240
I got to lose
that third dimension.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:57.740
-Right. Okay.

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:00.000
So maybe we could
summarize this box.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:01.625
Thank you, Cassidy.

00:10:01.625 --> 00:10:03.125
This is great.

00:10:03.125 --> 00:10:04.625
I don't usually get to see an
NDC box, because doesn't this

00:10:04.625 --> 00:10:07.085
happen on the GPU?

00:10:07.085 --> 00:10:08.605
-Yeah. This is inside the GPU.

00:10:08.605 --> 00:10:09.965
This is the memory that's

00:10:09.965 --> 00:10:11.325
actually going to end up 
on the screen.

00:10:11.325 --> 00:10:13.565
-Wow. And it's a tiny box.

00:10:13.565 --> 00:10:17.540
It only has a width, a height,
and a depth of two units.

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:19.940
-So it's one by
one by one by one.

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:21.440
-If you start middle.

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:23.240
Yes. The origin's in the middle.

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:24.740
It's left handed.

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:26.580
-Sorry. 
-That's okay.

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:28.080
We can figure it out.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:31.995
But to get to NDC space, you
start over in camera space.

00:10:31.995 --> 00:10:34.855
You do a projection matrix, a
perspective divide, you end up

00:10:34.855 --> 00:10:37.715
here, and then this is pretty
much the final jump of the

00:10:37.715 --> 00:10:40.575
rendering process, the pipeline,
I like to say,

00:10:40.575 --> 00:10:43.435
to jump over to
the screen.

00:10:43.435 --> 00:10:45.515
And you can kind of
see it coming together.

00:10:45.515 --> 00:10:47.180
Like it's ready to go.

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:48.680
It's just too small.

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:51.500
Just need to like scale it
up, move it a little bit.

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:53.420
Maybe in the next video, we'll
talk about how we can get this

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:55.340
to the screen.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:56.840
-Oh dear, okay.

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:58.340
That's hardware.